         -5,
         4
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Because h is a polynomial, it is continuous, so \\( \\lim_{x \\to 1} h(f(x)) = h(2) = 5 \\).",
         "rule_named": "limit of a composite with a continuous outer function"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The limit of the denominator is -4, which is not 0, so the quotient theorem applies.",
         "rule_named": "quotient limit theorem, denominator condition"
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "\\( \\lim_{x \\to 1} \\frac{h(f(x))}{g(x)} = \\frac{5}{-4} = - \\frac{5}{4} \\). The tabulated values at x = 1 are not used, because f and g are not continuous there.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            -5,
